ASSISTANT Tradeoffs to present:
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT - Option A: temp-dir copy + 9p share. Pros: simple, no image building, works with arbitrary sizes, host untouched. Cons: copy cost for large dirs, 9p performance.
ASSISTANT - Option B: disk image (qcow2/ext4) attached as virtio drive. Pros: fast inside VM, self-contained, easy to keep as artifact. Cons: need to build image at spawn (mkfs + populate), needs resize logic, more tooling.
ASSISTANT - Option C: squashfs (read-only) + overlayfs inside VM (upper layer on tmpfs or a small writable ext4). Pros: cheap snapshot, copy-on-write semantics — matches "modify without affecting host" elegantly; only changed files consume space. Cons: kernel needs overlayfs (fine on NixOS VM), squashfs tools needed at spawn.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT I'd recommend Option C (or B). Actually the requirement says "snapshot of the CWD should be taken" and "made available inside the QEMU vm" and "modify the contents without affecting files in the host". A CoW overlay is the cleanest: squashfs lower + writable upper.
